NAME
       find - search a directory tree for files that match tests

SYNOPSIS
       find [-H] [-L] [-P] [STARTING-POINT...] [EXPRESSION]

DESCRIPTION
       find walks down each starting point (the current directory when none
       is given) and checks every file and directory it meets against the
       expression. The expression is a list of tests (does the name match?
       is it owned by this user?) and actions (print it, delete it, run a
       command on it), read from left to right. Tests written next to each
       other are joined with "and", so an item counts only when every test
       is true.

       When the expression has no action, find prints the path of every match
       (as if -print were added at the end).

       Numbers in tests can be written three ways: +N means more than N, -N
       means less than N, and N means exactly N.

       find reports directories it cannot read on standard error. When you
       search the whole system as a normal user, add 2>/dev/null to hide
       those messages.

OPTIONS
   Options that change the walk (put them before the tests)
       -maxdepth N
              Go at most N levels below the starting point. -maxdepth 1 looks
              only at the starting directory's own entries.

       -mindepth N
              Ignore matches that are less than N levels deep.

       -xdev, -mount
              Stay on the file system of the starting point (do not walk into
              other mounted file systems).

       -L     Follow symbolic links.

   Tests
       -name PATTERN
              The file's name (not its directory) matches the shell pattern.
              Quote the pattern so the shell does not expand it: -name '*.conf'.

       -iname PATTERN
              Like -name, ignoring upper and lower case.

       -path PATTERN
              The whole path matches the pattern.

       -regex PATTERN
              The whole path matches the regular expression.

       -type C
              The kind of file: f regular file, d directory, l symbolic link,
              b block device, c character device, p named pipe, s socket.

       -user NAME, -uid N
              Owned by that user (name or number).

       -group NAME, -gid N
              Owned by that group.

       -nouser, -nogroup
              The owner (or group) number belongs to no existing account,
              for example after the user was deleted.

       -perm MODE
              Exactly these permission bits (-perm 644).

       -perm -MODE
              At least all of these bits are set (-perm -4000 finds set-user-ID
              programs, -perm -g=w finds group-writable files).

       -perm /MODE
              Any of these bits is set (-perm /u=s,g=s).

       -size [+-]N[ckMG]
              The size, rounded up to whole units: c bytes, k kibibytes,
              M mebibytes, G gibibytes. Without a unit, N counts 512-byte
              blocks. -size +10M means bigger than 10 MiB.

       -mtime [+-]N
              Contents changed N days ago (counted in whole 24-hour periods).
              -mtime -1 means within the last day.

       -mmin [+-]N
              Contents changed N minutes ago.

       -atime, -amin, -ctime, -cmin
              The same for the last access and the last status change
              (owner, permissions, links).

       -newer FILE
              Changed more recently than FILE.

       -empty An empty file or an empty directory.

       -links N
              Has N hard links.

       -inum N
              Has inode number N (finds all hard links to one file).

       -samefile FILE
              Is the same file as FILE (another hard link to it).

       -context PATTERN
              The SELinux context matches the pattern.

       -readable, -writable, -executable
              The user running find may read, write or run it.

   Operators
       ! TEST, -not TEST
              True when TEST is false.

       TEST1 -o TEST2, TEST1 -or TEST2
              True when either test is true.

       \( ... \)
              Group tests. Escape the parentheses from the shell.

   Actions
       -print Print the path, then a newline (the default action).

       -print0
              Print the path followed by a NUL byte, for xargs -0.

       -ls    Print a line like ls -dils for each match.

       -printf FORMAT
              Print with a format: %p path, %f name, %s size, %u owner,
              %g group, %m octal permissions, \n newline.

       -delete
              Delete the match. Try the expression with -print first.

       -exec COMMAND {} \;
              Run COMMAND once for every match; {} is replaced with the path.
              The \; (or ';') ends the command.

       -exec COMMAND {} +
              Run COMMAND as few times as possible, with many paths at once.

       -ok COMMAND {} \;
              Like -exec, but ask before each run.

       -prune Do not go into this directory.

       -quit  Stop at the first match.

EXAMPLES
       Find every file owned by the user student and copy it to /root/found,
       keeping the file attributes:

           mkdir -p /root/found
           find / -user student -type f -exec cp -a {} /root/found/ \;

       Find files larger than 30 MiB under /usr:

           find /usr -type f -size +30M

       Find all set-user-ID programs:

           find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null

       List configuration files in /etc changed in the last two days, without
       looking into subdirectories:

           find /etc -maxdepth 1 -name '*.conf' -mtime -2

       Save the paths of files under /home that no longer belong to any user:

           find /home -nouser > /root/orphans.txt

       Remove empty directories under /tmp/work:

           find /tmp/work -type d -empty -delete

NOTES
       Put -maxdepth and -mindepth right after the starting points; find warns
       when they come after tests.

       When a task asks you to save a list of files, redirect find's output to
       that file and check it with cat or wc -l afterwards.

SEE ALSO
       locate(1), xargs(1), ls(1), stat(1), chmod(1)
